This booklet supports the implementation of the skin NTD framework and has been developed primarily for front-line health-care workers like you, working in remote and underserved communities where skin NTDs and other skin conditions commonly co-exist. It provides clear, practical guidance to help you recognize and manage both skin NTDs and other frequently encountered skin diseases. The content follows a skin sign and symptom-based approach - starting from observable signs in the patient (such as a lump, ulcer, or itching)—to guide identification of potential conditions and inform appropriate management and referral. This method allows you to apply your knowledge even in situations where the exact diagnosis may not be clear. With experience, you will become increasingly confident in recognizing the signs and symptoms of skin diseases.

How can this booklet be used?

This booklet can be used for:

e@ Improving your understanding of skin diseases in general;

Improving your understanding of skin NTDs - including risk factors, signs of suspicion, reporting and public health implications;

e@

® Counselling of patients and their families - about treatment and prevention of skin diseases;

® Helping answer questions from your patients and their families;

@ Supporting you in providing treatment, making timely referrals, and following up with your patients; and

Supporting you in raising community awareness on how to prevent skin NTDs and other skin diseases, and to encourage timely care-seeking for skin conditions.

®

Clearly, there is a wide range of skin conditions, and it is not possible to include all of them in this booklet. However, the most common and clinically significant conditions have been carefully selected. This booklet is intended not only for your own reference, but also as a tool you can use to engage with patients, families, and communities. The photographs and illustrations provided can support health education efforts by helping to raise awareness and improve understanding of various skin diseases.

