2.1.3.2. Symptoms related to the peripheral nerve damage

There are not many skin diseases that present with peripheral nerve damage or enlargement, so if you find any symptoms suggestive of nerve damage or enlargement with a skin lesion, the diagnosis of leprosy becomes more likely. Peripheral nerve damage in leprosy is the cause of disabilities and deformities - the long-term consequences of the disease - and therefore, it is important to assess the presence of peripheral nerve damage not just for diagnosis but also for long-term follow-up on the disease.

The symptoms of peripheral nerve damage include:

@ numbness or tingling of hands and feet;

e dryness of the eyes or difficulty in closing the eyelids;

e weakness in the muscles;

e reduced sensation to the touch, pain and temperature (cold or hot);

e enlargement of the nerves;

e painful or tender nerves (“neuritis”).

Under normal conditions, peripheral nerves are neither visible nor palpable. However, in leprosy, affected nerves may become enlarged and can sometimes be seen or felt during physical examination. Conducting a thorough and careful sensory assessment is essential, as many patients in the early stages of leprosy may be unaware of their sensory loss.

